If you didn’t see my videos for the previous
episodes, this video will be a non-spoilery

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:27.890
review of his last vow. Then tomorrow I’ll
be doing a q&amp;a video where I answer specific

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:31.289
questions you guys have about the episode
and then the day after that I’ll post a

00:00:31.289 --> 00:00:35.390
detailed breakdown of the episode with all
the canon references and interesting facts

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:37.629
about the story. Be sure to subscribe to get
everything.

00:00:37.629 --> 00:00:42.500
Since this was the last episode I’ll also
be doing a series 4 video the same day I post

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:43.500
that breakdown video.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:48.000
So here are my top 5 favorite things about
His Last Vow. Number 5 - The Comedy. The episode

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.469
is a really intense drama, you have a villain,
sherlock and watson. and the threat that someone

00:00:53.469 --> 00:00:57.379
is definitely going to die at the end. The
actual pull quote from the trailer for the

00:00:57.379 --> 00:01:01.930
episode was “Sometimes someone has to die.”
But some of the funniest sherlock moments

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:04.239
come from the characters status as high functioning
sociopath.

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:08.251
A normal person like Watson gets really intense
during all the most dramatic moments, but

00:01:08.251 --> 00:01:12.090
somehow sherlock still manages to slip in
a subtle turn of phrase and it’s laugh out

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:13.130
loud funny.

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:17.220
On the other side of that coin, some of the
funniest moments in the episode come when

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:21.090
character express geniune sentiment towards
Sherlock and he recoils in fear.

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:23.110
I feel like it’s only a matter of time before
we see a high functioning sociopath stand

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:24.110
up comedy tour. It will just be sherlock walking
up to people saying awful things.

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:27.670
Number 4 - Mycroft. He’s one of the strangest
holmes characters. And even though he’s

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:31.780
supposed to be very emotionally distant. Mark
Gatiss still manages to surprise you with

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:36.260
some warmth and genuine emotion. There were
some amazing scenes between sherlock and mycroft

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:41.810
that invoked a lot of holmes boys comedy.
The episode gives you a lot of context for

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:44.370
why Mycroft behaves the way he does.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:49.500
There were a few scenes that I actually though
Mark Gatiss stole from Benedict Cumberbatch,

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:53.780
that’s how good he was. Typically people
only see sherlock when he’s on screen, so

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:58.090
you can forget sometimes that Mark Gatiss
is largely responsible for the dialogue given

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:06.439
to cumberbatch. So he’s really the most
clever person on the set.

00:02:06.439 --> 00:02:11.290
Number 3 - The canon references. This episode
has way more canon than empty hearse or sign

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:15.560
of three. Even if you haven’t read any of
the novels recently you get a sense of the

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:20.170
importance of places and events. One of the
things I really like about adapted stories

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:27.620
is the opportunity to explore episodes over
time. Nothing in the series is included in

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:34.040
the scripts or things in the set design and
locations without subtle meaning.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:38.150
Sherlock had this really amazing line in Sign
of Three where he says the universe is rarely

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:40.849
so lazy. And it’s just a metaphor for the
idea that everything is connected and there

00:02:40.849 --> 00:02:44.680
are no coincidences.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:49.810
Number 2 - The Villain. Lars Mikkelsen is
definitely a worthy successor to Andrew Scott’s

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:53.601
Moriarty. Holmes cannon is so vast that there’s
always more villains to pull off the page,

00:02:53.601 --> 00:02:57.680
but it’s really the actors that are responsible
for making them feel like a credible threat

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:58.790
to Sherlock.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:02.180
One of the differences between the Magnussen
character and the moriarty character that

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:07.069
I really liked was the absence of passion.
Moriarty was a force of pure emotion. He was

00:03:07.069 --> 00:03:11.239
the little kid on the playground that just
liked smashing other kids toys. But magnussen

00:03:11.239 --> 00:03:16.390
is a self-described businessman. Just acquiring
and selling assets. If we’re living in the

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:19.540
information age, then he’s like the ultimate
stock trader.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:24.120
and my number 1 favorite - the hero’s journey.
What I mean when I say that is Sherlock’s

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:29.980
journey. It’s the same journey from episode
1 to now. Sherlock in exile or hell clawing

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:34.659
his way back to heaven. His version of heaven
anyway. I saw a lot of you guys commenting

00:03:34.659 --> 00:03:38.230
on the big changes sherlock was going through
in each episode this series. I just think

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:44.640
it was sherlock slowly healing himself. He
was broken, team sherlock was broken and it

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:50.110
takes an entire series to put everything back
together so they can go on and have more adventures.

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:54.200
The ending of this episode in particular gives
you the sense that there will always be more

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:58.489
sherlock, because we as fans need him. and
Because in the context of the story england

00:03:58.489 --> 00:03:59.540
will always need him.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:03.780
I guess that does make him kind of a time
lord. Just regenerating himself every time

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:05.659
one adventure ends and another begins.

00:04:05.659 --> 00:04:09.560
Now it’s your turn to let me know what your
favorite part of the episode was. Was it the

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:13.689
plot or the surprise ending or all the funny
moments.

00:04:13.689 --> 00:04:17.780
Overall I thought the episode was brilliant
as expected. It added a lot more context for

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:24.030
the core relationships between Sherlock and
Mycroft and Watson and Mary’s relationship.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:28.150
I’m always amazed by how good the new actors
are each season.

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:33.040
Amanda Abbington was awesome. And Lars Mikkelsen
was so much fun to watch. I kept having Doctor

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:37.250
Who flashbacks based on his mannerisms and
abilities. I’ll talk more about that during

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:41.660
my breakdown video, but a lot of the credit
goes to redbeard for writing such good characters.

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:46.990
I mean Mark Gatiss. He and steven Moffat are
both responsible for casting, and they said

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:50.120
that they knew mikkelsen was the best choice
for the new villain from the moment they saw

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:51.120
him.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:54.220
Even though the episode was really intense
it left me with this really strong sense of

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:59.660
optimism for the future of the core characters
and the series in general. To be fair though,

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:04.060
steven moffat already started talking about
series 5. So there was no danger of sherlock

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:05.060
ever going away anytime soon.

